# Release train: <service>

Use a release train only when deployment cannot be continuous (fixed customer
windows, coordinated mobile or partner releases, regulated change boards).
Prefer small, independent deploys whenever the constraint allows it.

## Cadence

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Train interval | for example every Tuesday |
| Cut-off (last merge to trunk) | for example Monday 12:00 UTC |
| Candidate built from | trunk head at cut-off; one digest per train |
| Promotion window to production | for example Tuesday 09:00-12:00 UTC |
| Freeze periods | dates and reason |
| Train conductor (rotating) | name |

## Rules

1. Work merges to trunk behind flags; the train never waits for a feature.
2. The candidate is the digest CI built at cut-off. Nothing is rebuilt for the
   train; a fix means a new trunk commit and a new digest.
3. A missed train is not an emergency. The change rides the next one.
4. Hotfix releases follow the same pipeline (build once, promote through every
   environment) with shortened bake times recorded on the deploy gate
   checklist, never a side channel.
5. Release branches are cut only when a supported older version needs a fix;
   fixes land on trunk first and are cherry-picked.

## Per train record

| Train | Digest | Commits | Promoted at | Outcome | Rollback | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 2026-W44 | `sha256:...` | link | | success / rolled back | | |

## Exit criteria for the train model

Review every quarter: when lead time, deploy frequency, and change fail rate
show that smaller deploys would be safe, shorten the interval or move to
continuous deployment.
